Chapter 3 — Viability Kernels

3.1 The Viability Kernel

Definition 3.1.1. Let $\mathcal{K} \subseteq \mathcal{X}$ be an admissible set. A trajectory $\tau : [0, H] \to \mathcal{X}$ is viable if $\tau(t) \in \mathcal{K}$ for all $t \in [0, H]$.

Definition 3.1.2 (Aubin). The viability kernel of $\mathcal{K}$ under $F$ is
\mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K}) = \{x \in \mathcal{K} \mid \exists \text{ a viable trajectory from } x \text{ of duration } H\}.

Theorem 3.1.3 (Aubin's viability theorem, discrete form). Assume $\mathcal{K}$ is closed and $F$ is continuous. Then $\mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})$ is the largest closed subset $\mathcal{K}' \subseteq \mathcal{K}$ such that for every $x \in \mathcal{K}'$, there exists $u \in \mathcal{U}$ with $F(x, u, \xi) \in \mathcal{K}'$ for all $\xi \in \Xi$.

Proof. Standard fixed-point argument on the map $\Psi(\mathcal{K}') = \{x \in \mathcal{K}' \mid \exists u : F(x, u, \cdot) \in \mathcal{K}'\}$. Monotone on the complete lattice of closed subsets, so Knaster–Tarski applies. $\square$

3.2 Dead States and Recovery

Definition 3.2.1. A state $x \in \mathcal{K}$ is dead if no viable trajectory of positive duration starts from $x$:
\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{dead}} = \mathcal{K} \setminus \mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K}).

Proposition 3.2.2. If $x \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{dead}}$ and $x' \rightsquigarrow x$, then $x'$ may still be viable if there exists an alternative trajectory avoiding $x$.

Proof. Deadness is a property of the trajectory, not of the state itself. A state $x$ is dead only relative to the trajectories available. If another trajectory from $x'$ exists avoiding $x$, $x'$ remains viable. $\square$

3.3 Reachable Viable Set

Definition 3.3.1. The reachable viable set from $x$ over horizon $H$ is
\mathcal{R}_H(x) = \{x' \mid x \rightsquigarrow x' \text{ and } x' \in \mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})\}.

Proposition 3.3.2. $\mathcal{R}_H$ is monotone in $H$: $H_1 \le H_2 \implies \mathcal{R}_{H_1}(x) \subseteq \mathcal{R}_{H_2}(x)$.

Proof. Direct from the definition: any trajectory of duration $H_1$ extends to a trajectory of duration $H_2$ if the same viability holds. $\square$

Theorem 3.3.3. A state $x$ is viable for horizon $H$ iff $x \in \mathcal{R}_H(x')$ for some $x'$ that is viable at horizon $H+1$.

Proof. Recursive unfolding of the definition. $\square$

3.4 Distributed Viability

Definition 3.4.1. For a state field $x \in \mathcal{X}(\Omega)$, the distributed viability kernel is a subset
\mathcal{K}_H \subseteq \mathcal{X}(\Omega)


such that every $x \in \mathcal{K}_H$ admits a viable trajectory in the full field space.

Proposition 3.4.2. The distributed viability kernel is not the product of local viability kernels in general.

Proof. Counterexample: consider a 1D field $x : [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ with dynamics $F$ coupling neighboring values. Let $\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{local}} = \{x \in \mathcal{X} \mid |x(r)| \le 1\}$. The product $\prod_r \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{local}}(r)$ contains fields with high-frequency oscillations. If $F$ is a diffusion operator, such oscillations may violate a gradient constraint, so the true viability kernel is strictly smaller than the product. $\square$

Corollary 3.4.3. Any theory of industrial viability must consider the global field structure, not a decomposition into independent local regions.

