PART II — CORE THEORY: UAMA-0.4

Chapter 5 — Capability-Constrained Transition Architecture

5.1 The UAMA-0.4 Structure

Definition 5.1.1. A UAMA-0.4 architecture is a tuple
\mathfrak{A} = (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{Y}, \mathcal{T}_U, \mathcal{K}_H, \phi, \Pi),


where:

· $\mathcal{X}$ is the manufacturing state space;
· $\mathcal{C}$ is the set of capabilities;
· $\mathcal{R}$ is the resource space;
· $\mathcal{Y}$ is the observation space;
· $\mathcal{T}_U \subseteq \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}$ is the transition graph;
· $\mathcal{K}_H \subseteq \mathcal{X}$ is the viability kernel at horizon $H$;
· $\phi : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Z}$ is the state abstraction;
· $\Pi$ is the scope (materials, scales, precisions, conditions).

The transition graph is:
\mathcal{T}_U(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{R}, H) = \{(x, x') \in \mathcal{X}^2 \mid \exists (c, \theta, u, r, \xi) : x' = F_c(x, u, \xi)\}.

5.2 The Fundamental Transition Equation

Axiom 5.2.1. Every capability produces a joint transition:
(x, u, r) \xrightarrow{c} (x', y, w),


with
x' = F_c(x, u, \xi), \qquad y = H_c(x, u, \xi, \eta), \qquad w = W_c(x, u, \xi).

Axiom 5.2.2. Degradation is accumulated:
D_c : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathcal{X}, \qquad x \mapsto D_c(x, u, t).

Proposition 5.2.3. The separation of $F_c$ and $H_c$ is a decomposition, not an ontological separation. Both may be coupled, as in destructive observation.

Proof. From Proposition 1.3.3, any destructive observation can be represented as a joint transition. Conversely, a joint transition may be decomposed into $F_c$ and $H_c$ if the observation does not affect the state. $\square$

5.3 Locality

Definition 5.3.1. A capability $c$ is local if its precondition map $Pre(c)$ is defined pointwise in $\Omega$:
Pre(c)(\mathbf{r}) \subseteq \mathcal{C} \quad \forall \mathbf{r} \in \Omega.

Definition 5.3.2. A capability $c$ is non-local if its precondition depends on distant states:
Pre(c)(\mathbf{r}) \text{ depends on } x(\mathbf{r}') \text{ for } \mathbf{r}' \neq \mathbf{r}.

Proposition 5.3.3. Non-locality arises from:

· diffusion (thermal, chemical);
· mechanical stress propagation;
· electromagnetic coupling;
· contamination.

Proof. Direct from physical conservation laws in each case. $\square$

Corollary 5.3.4. The precondition triple must be indexed by position:
Pre(c, \mathbf{r}) = \bigl(Pre_{\mathrm{hard}}(c, \mathbf{r}), Pre_{\mathrm{soft}}(c, \mathbf{r}), Pre_{\mathrm{cond}}(c, \theta, \mathbf{r})\bigr).

5.4 Compatibility with Locality

Definition 5.4.1. Two capabilities $c_1, c_2$ are locally compatible if for all $\mathbf{r} \in \Omega$,
Compat(c_1, c_2, \theta_1, \theta_2, x)(\mathbf{r}) = 1.

Definition 5.4.2. Two capabilities are globally compatible if compatibility holds for the joint field.

Proposition 5.4.3. Local compatibility does not imply global compatibility.

Proof. Consider two adjacent regions $\Omega_1, \Omega_2$ where each is locally compatible but their boundary is not (e.g., thermal budget conflict at the interface). $\square$

