Chapter 6 — Irreversibility

6.1 The Transition Graph

Definition 6.1.1. The UAMA transition graph is $\mathcal{T}_U = (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{E})$ with
\mathcal{E} = \{(x, x') \mid \exists c \in \mathcal{C}, \theta, u, r, \xi : x' = F_c(x, u, \xi)\}.

Proposition 6.1.2. $\mathcal{T}_U$ is a directed graph (digraph). It may contain cycles.

Theorem 6.1.3. The reachability preorder on $\mathcal{T}_U$ induces a poset on the strongly connected components (SCCs) of $\mathcal{T}_U$.

Proof. Condensation theorem for digraphs. $\square$

6.2 Irreversibility as a Graph Property

Definition 6.2.1. An edge $(x, x') \in \mathcal{E}$ is irreversible in $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{R}, H)$ if $x$ and $x'$ lie in different SCCs with no path from $x'$ back to $x$.

Theorem 6.2.2. The SCC condensation of $\mathcal{T}_U$ is a DAG. Every edge in the DAG is irreversible in the sense of Definition 6.2.1.

Proof. Direct from the condensation theorem. $\square$

Corollary 6.2.3. Irreversibility is a topological property of the transition graph, not of individual states.

6.3 Recovery Cost as Pareto Vector

Definition 6.3.1. The recovery cost of $x'$ from $x$ is the set
R(x, x') \subseteq \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^k \cup \{\infty\},


where $k$ is the number of cost dimensions.

Proposition 6.3.2. The recovery cost is monotonically non-decreasing in the horizon $H$:
H_1 \le H_2 \implies R_{H_1}(x, x') \supseteq R_{H_2}(x, x')


(in the Pareto sense).

Proof. Longer horizons permit more recovery trajectories, hence a lower cost Pareto front. $\square$

Definition 6.3.3. The state $x$ is recoverable from $x'$ if $R(x, x') \neq \{\infty\}$.

6.4 Recovery as a Resource

Theorem 6.4.1. Recovery capability itself consumes resources. If $\mathcal{R}$ is bounded, then not all reversible transitions can be executed.

Proof. By resource conservation, executing the recovery trajectory consumes resources from $\mathcal{R}$. If $\mathcal{R}$ is finite, only finitely many recoveries are possible. $\square$

Corollary 6.4.2. A viable architecture must reserve resources for recovery, not just for production.

