PART III — THE MVIK PROBLEM

Chapter 9 — Formalization

9.1 Definition

Definition 9.1.1. The Minimum Viable Industrial Kernel (MVIK) at closure level $\Gamma_{\min}$, external dependency $D_{\max}$, horizon $H$, and scope $\Pi$ is the Pareto front:
\mathrm{MVIK}(\Gamma_{\min}, D_{\max}, H, \Pi) = \mathrm{Pareto}\bigl\{(V, E, I) \mid \exists \mathcal{C}^* \subseteq \mathcal{C} : \text{constraints hold}\bigr\},


where the constraints are:

1. $P(\mathcal{C}^*) \ge P_{\min}$ (productive capacity);
2. $\Gamma_C(\Pi, H) \ge \Gamma_{\min}$ (capacity closure);
3. $\Gamma_R(\Pi, H) \ge \Gamma_{R,\min}$ (recovery closure);
4. $D_{\mathrm{ext}}(\mathcal{C}^*) \le D_{\max}$ (external dependency);
5. $\exists \tau : [0, H] \to \mathcal{X}$ with $\tau(t) \in \mathcal{K}_H$ (viability);
6. $Q(\mathcal{C}^*) \ge Q_{\min}$ (quality).

9.2 Existence

Theorem 9.2.1. If $\mathcal{C}$ is finite, then $\mathrm{MVIK}$ is non-empty for any $\Gamma_{\min}, D_{\max}$ satisfying $\Gamma_{\min} \le 1$ and $D_{\max} \ge 0$ with $\mathcal{C}$ viable at those levels.

Proof. The set $\mathcal{C}$ is finite, so $2^{\mathcal{C}}$ is finite. The constraint set is a subset of $2^{\mathcal{C}} \times \mathbb{R}^3$ and is closed in the product topology. The Pareto front is finite and non-empty by well-ordering. $\square$

Theorem 9.2.2. If $\mathcal{C}$ is infinite (e.g., $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^n$), existence requires compactness of the constraint set.

Proof. Without compactness, the Pareto front may be empty (e.g., if the constraint set is open). $\square$

9.3 Uniqueness

Proposition 9.3.1. MVIK is generally not unique.

Proof. By Theorem 2.2.4, the closure under competition has multiple fixed points. Each fixed point yields a different MVIK. $\square$

Corollary 9.3.2. MVIK is a set, not a point.

9.4 The Volume Function

Definition 9.4.1. The volume function is
V_{\min}(\Gamma_C, D_{\max}, P_{\min}, R_{\min}, A_{\min}, Q_{\min}, E_{\max}) = \min \{V(\mathcal{C}^*) \mid \mathcal{C}^* \text{ satisfies constraints}\}.

Theorem 9.4.2. $V_{\min}$ is monotonically non-decreasing in $\Gamma_C$ under the assumption that higher closure requires more capabilities.

Proof. By definition, higher $\Gamma_C$ requires more renewable critical capabilities, hence larger $\mathcal{C}^*$. If the capabilities are non-substitutable, $V$ increases. $\square$

Remark 9.4.3. The assumption of non-substitutability may fail in modular designs, where reconfiguration allows reuse of physical space. In that case $V_{\min}$ may be flat or decreasing in $\Gamma_C$.

9.5 The Volume Minimization Problem

Definition 9.5.1. The volume minimization problem is
V_{\min} = \min_{\tau} \max_{t \in [0, H]} V(t),


where $\tau$ ranges over viable trajectories and $V(t)$ is the spatial volume at time $t$.

Theorem 9.5.2. If the trajectory space is compact and $V$ is continuous, the minimum is attained.

Proof. Weierstrass theorem. $\square$

Corollary 9.5.3. The MVIK is a minimax problem over viable trajectories.

