Chapter 10 — Pareto-Constrained Architecture Synthesis

10.1 Synthesis Problem

Definition 10.1.1. The architecture synthesis problem is:
\mathcal{A}^* = \arg\min_{\mathcal{A}} \; V(\mathcal{A}) \quad \text{subject to} \quad (\Gamma_C, D_{\mathrm{ext}}, P, R, A, Q) \in \mathcal{F},


where $\mathcal{F}$ is the feasible set.

10.2 Multi-Objective Formulation

Definition 10.2.1. The Pareto front of the synthesis problem is
\mathcal{P} = \{\mathcal{A} \mid \nexists \mathcal{A}' : \mathcal{A}' \preceq \mathcal{A} \text{ and } \mathcal{A}' \neq \mathcal{A}\},


where $\preceq$ is componentwise order in $(V, E, I, D_{\mathrm{ext}}, \Gamma_C, \Gamma_R)$.

Theorem 10.2.2. Under compactness of $\mathcal{A}$, the Pareto front is non-empty.

Proof. By Zorn's lemma, any non-empty compact set has a maximal element under any partial order. $\square$

10.3 Structure of the Pareto Front

Proposition 10.3.1. The Pareto front has dimension at most $\dim(V, E, I, D_{\mathrm{ext}}, \Gamma_C, \Gamma_R) - 1$.

Proof. Standard result for Pareto fronts. $\square$

Theorem 10.3.2. The Pareto front is generically a $(k-1)$-dimensional manifold, where $k$ is the number of objectives.

Proof. Under smoothness and constraint qualification (KKT conditions), the Pareto front is a smooth manifold of dimension $k-1$. $\square$

