Chapter 11 — Volume Minimization Under Closure Constraints

11.1 The Fundamental Equation

Theorem 11.1.1 (Volume–Closure Trade-off). Under the assumption of non-substitutable capabilities:
V_{\min}(\Gamma_C) \ge V_0 + \alpha \Gamma_C


for some $\alpha > 0$, where $V_0$ is the volume of the minimum productive system.

Proof. Let $\mathcal{C}^*(\Gamma_C)$ be the closure-achieving set. By definition, $\Gamma_C$ is monotone in $|\mathcal{C}^*|$. If capabilities are non-substitutable, each additional capability requires positive volume. Hence $V$ is at least linear in $|\mathcal{C}^*|$, giving the bound. $\square$

11.2 Modularity and Reconfiguration

Definition 11.2.1. A capability set $\mathcal{C}$ is modular if it decomposes as $\mathcal{C} = \bigsqcup_i \mathcal{M}_i$ where each module $\mathcal{M}_i$ can be replaced by another module of the same class without redesigning the rest.

Theorem 11.2.2. For modular architectures:
V_{\min}(\Gamma_C) \le V_{\mathrm{mod}} + \beta \log(\Gamma_C)


for some $\beta > 0$ and $V_{\mathrm{mod}}$, under time-sharing of modules.

Proof. Time-sharing allows a single module to serve multiple functions, reducing the required volume. The $\log$ factor arises from the combinatorial number of shared resources. $\square$

Corollary 11.2.3. Modularity can transform the volume–closure relation from linear to logarithmic.

11.3 Temporal Volume

Definition 11.3.1. The temporal volume of a trajectory $\tau$ is
V_T(\tau) = \int_0^H V(\tau(t)) \, dt.

Theorem 11.3.2. The minimum temporal volume is achieved by a trajectory that concentrates high-volume activities in the early phase.

Proof. Under the assumption that early phases require less closure, front-loading high-volume activities reduces the integral. $\square$

