Chapter 15 — AI as Search Operator

15.1 Problem Formulation

Definition 15.1.1. The AI search problem is:
\mathcal{A}^* = \arg\min_{\mathcal{A} \in \mathcal{F}} J(\mathcal{A}),


where $J$ is a multi-objective criterion and $\mathcal{F}$ is the feasible set.

15.2 Simulator-in-the-Loop

Axiom 15.2.1. Physical simulation is the arbiter of validity.

Algorithm 15.2.2 (Simulator-in-the-Loop Search).

```
1. Initialize candidate A_0.
2. Loop:
   a. Propose A_{t+1} = G_θ(A_t, D_t, C).
   b. Simulate A_{t+1} under constraints.
   c. Compute (V, E, I, Γ_C, Γ_R, D_ext, P, Q).
   d. If A_{t+1} is not feasible, reject.
   e. Otherwise, add to Pareto front.
3. Return Pareto front.
```

Theorem 15.2.3. Under sufficient exploration, Algorithm 15.2.2 converges to the Pareto front in the limit.

Proof. Standard argument from stochastic approximation: with infinite samples and sufficient exploration, the empirical Pareto front converges to the true Pareto front. $\square$

15.3 The Architecture Search Space

Definition 15.3.1. The architecture search space is
\mathcal{A} = (\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{X}, \mathcal{R}, \mathrm{layout}, \mathrm{schedule}, \mathrm{control}).

Proposition 15.3.2. The search space is exponentially large in $|\mathcal{C}|$.

Proof. Each capability can be included or not, giving $2^{|\mathcal{C}|}$ subsets. $\square$

Corollary 15.3.3. Exhaustive search is infeasible. Heuristic or learning-based search is necessary.

15.4 Non-degeneracy of AI

Theorem 15.4.1. AI is non-degenerate as a search operator iff it can distinguish architectures with different $(V, E, I, \Gamma_C, \Gamma_R, D_{\mathrm{ext}})$.

Proof. If two architectures are indistinguishable in the objective space, no search operator can distinguish them. Non-degeneracy requires the objective vector to be injective on the Pareto-relevant architectures. $\square$

