APPENDIX C — VIABILITY KERNEL THEORY: FULL PROOFS

C.1 Discrete Viability

Theorem C.1.1 (Aubin, discrete). Let $\mathcal{K} \subseteq \mathcal{X}$ be closed and $F : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \Xi \to \mathcal{X}$ continuous. The viability kernel $\mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})$ is the largest closed subset $\mathcal{K}' \subseteq \mathcal{K}$ satisfying
\forall x \in \mathcal{K}', \exists u \in \mathcal{U}, \forall \xi \in \Xi : F(x, u, \xi) \in \mathcal{K}'.

Proof. Define the map
\Psi(\mathcal{K}') = \{x \in \mathcal{K}' \mid \exists u : F(x, u, \cdot) \in \mathcal{K}'\}.


$\Psi$ is monotone on the complete lattice of closed subsets of $\mathcal{K}$ (under reverse inclusion, or using the standard fixpoint formulation). By Knaster–Tarski, $\Psi$ has a greatest fixed point, which is $\mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})$. $\square$

Theorem C.1.2. $\mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K}) = \bigcap_{n \ge 0} \mathcal{K}_n$, where $\mathcal{K}_0 = \mathcal{K}$ and
\mathcal{K}_{n+1} = \{x \in \mathcal{K}_n \mid \exists u : F(x, u, \cdot) \in \mathcal{K}_n\}.

Proof. By Kleene iteration on the dual lattice. $\square$

C.2 Continuous Viability

Theorem C.2.1 (Aubin, continuous). For a differential inclusion $\dot{x} \in F(x)$ with $F$ upper semicontinuous with compact convex values, the viability kernel $\mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})$ satisfies:
x \in \mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K}) \iff F(x) \cap T_{\mathcal{K}}(x) \neq \emptyset,


where $T_{\mathcal{K}}(x)$ is the contingent cone to $\mathcal{K}$ at $x$.

Proof. By the viability theorem of Aubin and Cellina. $\square$

C.3 Distributed Viability

Definition C.3.1. For $\mathcal{X}(\Omega)$ with $\Omega \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$, the distributed viability kernel is
\mathcal{K}_H = \{x \in \mathcal{X}(\Omega) \mid \exists \text{ viable trajectory of duration } H\}.

Theorem C.3.2. In general,
\mathcal{K}_H \neq \prod_{\mathbf{r} \in \Omega} \mathcal{K}_H^{\mathrm{local}}(\mathbf{r}),


where $\mathcal{K}_H^{\mathrm{local}}(\mathbf{r})$ is the viability kernel for the local state at $\mathbf{r}$ under decoupled dynamics.

Proof. Let $\mathcal{X}(\Omega) = L^2([0, 1])$ and $F$ be the heat equation with Dirichlet boundary conditions. The local constraint $\mathcal{K}^{\mathrm{local}} = \{|x(r)| \le 1\}$ admits product states with arbitrarily high-frequency oscillations, which violate the gradient constraint $\|\nabla x\|_\infty \le 1$ imposed by the heat equation. Hence the product is strictly larger than the true viability kernel. $\square$

Corollary C.3.3. Any correct theory of industrial viability must consider the joint field structure.

C.4 Reachable Viable Set

Definition C.4.1. The reachable viable set from $x$ is
\mathcal{R}_H(x) = \{x' \mid x \rightsquigarrow x' \text{ and } x' \in \mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})\}.

Theorem C.4.2. $\mathcal{R}_H$ is monotone in $H$:
H_1 \le H_2 \implies \mathcal{R}_{H_1}(x) \subseteq \mathcal{R}_{H_2}(x).

Proof. Any trajectory of duration $H_1$ extends to $H_2$ if the target state remains viable. $\square$

Theorem C.4.3. $\mathcal{R}_H(x)$ is closed under the viability condition: for any $x' \in \mathcal{R}_H(x)$, there exists a viable trajectory from $x'$ of duration $H$.

Proof. By definition of $\mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})$. $\square$

C.5 Dead States

Definition C.5.1. A state $x \in \mathcal{K}$ is dead if no viable trajectory of positive duration starts from $x$. The dead set is
\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{dead}} = \mathcal{K} \setminus \mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K}).

Theorem C.5.2. $\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{dead}}$ is closed.

Proof. $\mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})$ is closed (as a fixed point of a monotone operator on closed sets), and $\mathcal{K}$ is closed. $\square$

Theorem C.5.3. A state $x$ is dead iff for all $u \in \mathcal{U}$, there exists $\xi \in \Xi$ such that $F(x, u, \xi) \notin \mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})$.

Proof. By the characterization of $\mathrm{Viab}_F(\mathcal{K})$ from Theorem C.1.1. $\square$

Corollary C.5.4. Deadness is a robust property: it holds regardless of the control choice.

