D.1 Full Capability Definition

Definition D.1.1. A capability is a 13-tuple
c = (P, \theta, x, u, r, I, O, W, F_c, H_c, D_c, Pre, Compat),


where:

· $P \in \mathcal{P}$ is a primitive;
· $\theta \in \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p$ is a parameter vector;
· $x \in \mathcal{X}$ is the state;
· $u \in \mathcal{U}$ is the control;
· $r \in \mathcal{R}$ is the resource vector;
· $I, O, W$ are input, output, waste specifications;
· $F_c : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \Xi \to \mathcal{X}$ is the state transition;
· $H_c : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \Xi \to \mathcal{Y}$ is the observation;
· $D_c : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathcal{X}$ is the degradation;
· $Pre = (Pre_{\mathrm{hard}}, Pre_{\mathrm{soft}}, Pre_{\mathrm{cond}})$ is the precondition triple;
· $Compat : \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{C} \times \Theta \times \Theta \times \mathcal{X} \to \{0, 1\}$ is the compatibility predicate.

D.2 Primitive Set

Definition D.2.1. The canonical primitive set is
\mathcal{P} = \{\mathrm{Sep}, \mathrm{Add}, \mathrm{Form}, \mathrm{Asm}, \mathrm{Trans}, \mathrm{Syn}, \mathrm{Meas}, \mathrm{Ctrl}, \mathrm{Conv}, \mathrm{Store}, \mathrm{Move}, \mathrm{Maint}\}.

Axiom D.2.2 (Primitive invariance). No new primitive is added unless a phenomenon cannot be expressed by the existing structure.

Theorem D.2.3. The primitive set is closed under composition: if $P_1, P_2 \in \mathcal{P}$, then $P_2 \circ P_1$ is expressible as a capability using primitives in $\mathcal{P}$.

Proof. Any composition is a sequence of primitive operations, each expressible using the twelve primitives. $\square$

D.3 Precondition Typology

Definition D.3.1. The three precondition types:

· $Pre_{\mathrm{hard}}(c) \subseteq \mathcal{C}$: physically necessary;
· $Pre_{\mathrm{soft}}(c) \subseteq \mathcal{C}$: practically necessary;
· $Pre_{\mathrm{cond}}(c, \theta) \subseteq \mathcal{C}$: conditionally necessary.

Theorem D.3.2. For a capability $c$ to be realizable in state $S \subseteq \mathcal{C}$:
Pre_{\mathrm{hard}}(c) \subseteq S \wedge Pre_{\mathrm{soft}}(c) \subseteq S \wedge Pre_{\mathrm{cond}}(c, \theta) \subseteq S.

Proof. By definition. $\square$

Proposition D.3.3. $Pre_{\mathrm{hard}}$, $Pre_{\mathrm{soft}}$, $Pre_{\mathrm{cond}}$ are not in general nested.

Proof. Counterexample: soldering requires atmospheric control (hard) but not always flux (soft); flux may be conditional on the alloy (cond). The three sets can be pairwise incomparable. $\square$

D.4 Compatibility Relation

Definition D.4.1. The compatibility predicate satisfies:

· Reflexivity: $Compat(c, c, \theta, \theta, x) = 1$ for all $c, \theta, x$;
· Symmetry: $Compat(c_1, c_2, \theta_1, \theta_2, x) = Compat(c_2, c_1, \theta_2, \theta_1, x)$;
· Non-transitivity: $Compat(c_1, c_2, \cdot) = Compat(c_2, c_3, \cdot) = 1 \not\Rightarrow Compat(c_1, c_3, \cdot) = 1$.

Theorem D.4.2. The compatibility relation is a graph, not an equivalence.

Proof. Symmetry and reflexivity give a graph, but non-transitivity prevents an equivalence structure. $\square$

Proposition D.4.3. Compatibility depends on state $x$ in general.

Proof. Two operations may be compatible in a clean state and incompatible in a contaminated state (e.g., lithography after deposition). $\square$

D.5 Degradation Dynamics

Definition D.5.1. The degradation function $D_c : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathcal{X}$ satisfies:

· $D_c(x, u, 0) = x$ (identity);
· $D_c(x, u, t)$ is continuous in $t$;
· $D_c$ is monotone in the sense of increasing entropy or decreasing capability.

Theorem D.5.2. If $D_c$ is Lipschitz in $t$ with constant $L_D$, then the accumulated degradation over horizon $H$ is bounded by $L_D H$.

Proof. Direct. $\square$

Definition D.5.3. The degradation rate is
\dot{D}_c(x, u) = \lim_{h \to 0^+} \frac{D_c(x, u, h) - x}{h}.

Proposition D.5.4. For a system with degradation rate $\dot{D}_c$, the maintenance interval is
\tau_{\mathrm{maint}} = \frac{\epsilon}{|\dot{D}_c|},


where $\epsilon$ is the tolerance.

Proof. Direct. $\square$

