APPENDIX F — STATE SUFFICIENCY: COMPLETE ANALYSIS

F.1 State Abstraction

Definition F.1.1. A state abstraction is a map $\phi : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Z}$.

Definition F.1.2. The $\phi$-equivalence is $x_1 \sim_\phi x_2 \iff \phi(x_1) = \phi(x_2)$.

F.2 Trajectorial Sufficiency

Definition F.2.1. $\phi$ is $(\mathcal{C}_F, H, Q)$-trajectorially sufficient if for all $x_1, x_2 \in \mathcal{X}$ with $\phi(x_1) = \phi(x_2)$,
Q(\tau^c_{x_1}) = Q(\tau^c_{x_2}) \quad \forall c \in \mathcal{C}_F, \forall \tau \text{ of duration } H.

Theorem F.2.2. Trajectorial sufficiency is monotone in $H$: if $\phi$ is $(H_1)$-sufficient, it is $(H_0)$-sufficient for $H_0 \le H_1$.

Proof. Restrict to shorter trajectories. $\square$

F.3 Decisional Sufficiency

Definition F.3.1. $\phi$ is $(\mathcal{C}_F, H, Q, \pi^*)$-decisionally sufficient if for all $x_1, x_2$ with $\phi(x_1) = \phi(x_2)$,
\pi^*(x_1) = \pi^*(x_2).

Theorem F.3.2. Decisional sufficiency is strictly weaker than trajectorial sufficiency.

Proof. Any two states with the same trajectory satisfy the same decision. The converse fails: two states can lead to different trajectories but the same optimal decision (e.g., both lead to scrap). $\square$

Counterexample F.3.3. Let $x_1, x_2$ differ in a parameter that affects only a non-critical quality metric. Both are optimal to accept. Trajectorial sufficiency fails, decisional sufficiency holds.

F.4 Approximate Sufficiency

Definition F.4.1. $\phi$ is $(\mathcal{C}_F, H, Q, \epsilon)$-sufficient if for all $x_1, x_2$ with $\phi(x_1) = \phi(x_2)$,
\sup_{c \in \mathcal{C}_F} d_Q(Q(\tau^c_{x_1}), Q(\tau^c_{x_2})) \le \epsilon.

Theorem F.4.2. $\epsilon$-sufficiency is monotone in $\epsilon$.

Proof. Direct. $\square$

F.5 Composition Sufficiency

Definition F.5.1. The composition $c_2 \circ c_1$ is defined when
Compat(c_2, c_1, \theta_2, \theta_1, F_{c_1}(x, u_1, \xi_1)) = 1.

Theorem F.5.2 (Composition sufficiency). If $\phi$ is $(\{c_2\}, H, Q)$-sufficient, then $c_2 \circ c_1$ is computable from $\phi(F_{c_1}(x, u, \xi))$.

Proof. By sufficiency, all states $\phi$-equivalent to $F_{c_1}(x, u, \xi)$ yield the same observations under $c_2$. $\square$

Corollary F.5.3. Composition requires sufficiency at each intermediate state.

F.6 Self-Referential Sufficiency

Definition F.6.1. A sufficiency fixed point is a pair $(\phi, \mathcal{C}_F)$ such that:

1. $\phi$ is $(\mathcal{C}_F, H, Q)$-sufficient;
2. $\mathcal{C}_F$ is generated by the architecture using $\phi$.

Theorem F.6.2. Sufficiency fixed points exist under:

· $\mathcal{C}_F$ monotone in $\phi$;
· the sufficiency condition defines a closed subset;
· the abstraction space is compact.

Proof. Apply Schauder's theorem to the map $\phi \mapsto$ set of $\mathcal{C}_F(\phi)$-sufficient $\phi'$. $\square$

Corollary F.6.3. Uniqueness requires additional conditions (e.g., strict convexity).

F.7 The Compactness Constraint

Definition F.7.1. The state representation cost is
C_{\mathrm{state}}(\phi) = (C_{\mathrm{sense}}, C_{\mathrm{comp}}, C_{\mathrm{store}}, C_{\mathrm{cal}}, C_{\mathrm{energy}}, C_{\mathrm{vol}}).

Axiom F.7.2. $C_{\mathrm{state}}(\phi)$ is monotone non-decreasing in the information content of $\phi$.

Definition F.7.3. $\phi$ is non-degenerate if for each component of $\phi$, there exists a capability $c \in \mathcal{C}_F$ and $x_1, x_2$ with $\phi(x_1) = \phi(x_2)$ but $F_c$ distinguishable.

F.8 The SS-C Conjecture

Conjecture F.8.1 (State Sufficiency–Compactness). For a given class of manufacturing capabilities, an architecture can be compositionally predictive only if the state abstraction $\phi$ preserved after each transition is sufficient for the relevant consequences of future capabilities within the scope.

Falsification criterion. SS-C fails for a class $\mathcal{C}$ if there exists a process in $\mathcal{C}$ such that no $\phi$ satisfies all three:

1. $\epsilon$-sufficient for all $\epsilon < \infty$;
2. non-degenerate;
3. $C_{\mathrm{state}}(\phi) \le C_{\max}$.

Theorem F.8.2. Each of the four mechanisms M1–M4 produces a distinct failure mode.

Proof. M1 (long memory) fails at dimensionality. M2 (chaotic amplification) fails at precision. M3 (emergent defects) fails at aggregation. M4 (non-local correlations) fails at locality. Each requires different refinement. $\square$

