APPENDIX G — MVIK: FULL EXISTENCE AND COMPUTABILITY

G.1 Formal Definition

Definition G.1.1. The MVIK at $(\Gamma_{\min}, D_{\max}, H, \Pi)$ is
\mathrm{MVIK} = \mathrm{Pareto}\{(V, E, I) \mid \exists \mathcal{C}^* \subseteq \mathcal{C}\}


subject to:

1. $P(\mathcal{C}^*) \ge P_{\min}$;
2. $\Gamma_C(\Pi, H) \ge \Gamma_{\min}$;
3. $\Gamma_R(\Pi, H) \ge \Gamma_{R,\min}$;
4. $D_{\mathrm{ext}}(\mathcal{C}^*) \le D_{\max}$;
5. $\exists \tau$ viable;
6. $Q(\mathcal{C}^*) \ge Q_{\min}$.

G.2 Existence

Theorem G.2.1. If $\mathcal{C}$ is finite, MVIK is non-empty.

Proof. $2^\mathcal{C}$ is finite. The feasible set is closed. The Pareto front is finite and non-empty. $\square$

Theorem G.2.2. If $\mathcal{C}$ is infinite, existence requires compactness.

Proof. Without compactness, the constraint set may be open or unbounded. $\square$

G.3 Uniqueness

Theorem G.3.1. MVIK is generally not unique.

Proof. Multiple closure fixed points (Theorem B.2.6) give multiple MVIK. $\square$

Theorem G.3.2. Uniqueness requires strict convexity of the objective and singleton constraint set.

Proof. Standard convexity argument. $\square$

G.4 Volume Function

Definition G.4.1. $V_{\min}(\Gamma_C, \ldots) = \min\{V(\mathcal{C}^*) \mid \text{constraints hold}\}$.

Theorem G.4.2. $V_{\min}$ is monotone non-decreasing in $\Gamma_C$ under non-substitutability.

Proof. By definition, higher $\Gamma_C$ requires more capabilities. $\square$

Theorem G.4.3. Under modularity, $V_{\min}$ may be logarithmic in $\Gamma_C$.

Proof. Time-sharing of modules reduces required volume. $\square$

G.5 Temporal Volume

Definition G.5.1. $V_T(\tau) = \int_0^H V(\tau(t)) dt$.

Definition G.5.2. $V_{\min}^{\mathrm{dyn}} = \min_\tau \max_{t \in [0, H]} V(t)$.

Theorem G.5.3. $V_{\min}^{\mathrm{dyn}} \le V_{\min}^{\mathrm{static}}$.

Proof. Any static architecture is a special case of a dynamic trajectory. $\square$

G.6 Computability

Theorem G.6.1. MVIK is NP-hard.

Proof. Reduction from Set Cover. $\square$

Theorem G.6.2. Under submodularity, $(1 - 1/e)$-approximation is achievable.

Proof. By the standard greedy bound. $\square$

